  :receipt/date "2026-08-18"
  :source/dataset "receipt"
  :receipt/title "Getting the hydrate fix onto cypher, and the three source-paths that had stopped two builds"
  :receipt/body
  "The previous receipt landed the native outer-string decode in
   net-kotobase/control-plane and left one thing open: the same file lives in
   `kotoba-lang/kotobase-worker-shell`, which `cypher.kotobase.net` consumes by
   relative source-path. This closes that.

   ## The pin

   west pin `ba29bd6a -> f8305070`, a pure fast-forward (ahead 3, behind 0,
   merge-base == the old pin, checked with `gh api compare` before touching
   anything). Written with `scripts/west-pin-put.cljs`, which is the single-entry
   API path: exactly one line of a 1.05 MB generated file changed, with the blob
   SHA as precondition so a moved tip 409s instead of clobbering.

   I first tried to hand-roll the PUT. The PreToolUse hook refused it, correctly:
   it runs BEFORE the command, so generating the payload and PUTting it in one
   invocation leaves the hook reading a file that does not exist yet. The hook
   named the script to use instead.

   ## Three source-paths, two repos, and builds that had stopped

   Both `kotobase-worker-shell` and `net-kotobase/cypher` failed at COMPILE with
   `The required namespace ... is not available`. Three paths were missing from
   each `shadow-cljs.edn`, and each one surfaced only after the previous was
   added:

       kotobase.blockcodec.core        -> ../kotobase-block-codec/src
       deflate.core                    -> ../org-ietf-deflate/src
       kotobase.projection.statistics  -> ../kotobase-projection/src

   The shell's test suite therefore had not executed at all. With the paths
   restored it runs: **45 tests / 169 assertions / 0 failures**, and 48/196/0
   after porting the three `read-string-literal` tests from the repo the library
   was extracted from — so the fast path, the control-character fallback and the
   non-string guard are covered there and not only upstream.

   This is the same failure the workspace keeps naming: not a check that fails,
   a check that cannot run. Nothing was red. Nothing was anything.

   ## Verifying the change actually reached the bundle

   `read_string_literal` does not survive a shadow-cljs release build, so its
   absence from the bundle proves nothing. The discriminator was to build the
   same worker against the OLD shell pin in a contained worktree and diff:

       old pin  bundle 6172ef24   JSON.parse x4
       new pin  bundle a963d66d   JSON.parse x5

   One additional call — the change, and nothing else. The real checkout then
   reproduced `a963d66d` byte-for-byte before deploying.

   **I nearly skipped this twice.** Both times the resource guard rejected the
   comparison build with exit 2 because another session held the build lock, and
   both times the `cp` that followed silently copied the previous artifact, so
   the two files I was about to 'compare' were the same file. Caught by checking
   the hashes rather than trusting the copy. A comparison of a file with itself
   reports whatever noise is in the measurement as the effect.

   ## Deployed

   `net-kotobase-cypher` version `d00e45b5`. After: `/health` 200 in 0.11-0.13 s
   across three probes, an unauthenticated Cypher query still refused with 401,
   and `net-kotobase-live-planes` green at 20/20.

   ## What is NOT claimed

   **The effect on cypher was not measured in production.** The technique that
   worked for protocols-worker — `wrangler tail` paired with a hydrating request
   — needs a request that hydrates, and every cypher read returns 401 to a
   self-issued CACAO: it wants an operator CACAO, and
   `KOTOBASE_MAINTENANCE_OPERATOR_DIDS` is owner-blocked. What is measured is
   upstream (-28% of the fold phase on production blocks; production cpuTime
   median 2397 -> 2058 ms) plus the bundle diff proving the identical code is in
   what was deployed.

   ## Also found, not fixed

   **Two repositories deploy the same worker name to the same hostname**:
   `network-awai/net-kotobase-cypher` and `net-kotobase/cypher` both declare
   `name: net-kotobase-cypher` on `cypher.kotobase.net`. Last deploy wins, which
   is the shape of the 2026-07-25 kotobase.net funnel regression. They differ
   today only in README.md, so nothing was reverted here, and the synced one
   (`net-kotobase/cypher`, 0 ahead / 0 behind; the other is 2 behind) was used.
   That they agree today is luck, not design."
